🚀 Linux Quick Guide in 1 Day – Master the Basics! 🐧

Want to get started with Linux but short on time? Here’s a 1-day crash course to build strong fundamentals 👇

🔹 Morning (3–4 Hours) – Basics & Navigation

Understand Linux distributions (Ubuntu, CentOS, RHEL, Fedora).

Learn the file system hierarchy.

Master navigation commands: ls, cd, pwd, tree.

File operations: cat, less, touch, cp, mv, rm.


🔹 Afternoon (3–4 Hours) – User & System Management

File permissions: chmod, chown, umask.

Users & groups: useradd, usermod, passwd, groups.

Process management: ps, top, kill, jobs, bg, fg.

Package management → apt (Debian/Ubuntu), dnf (RHEL/Fedora).


🔹 Evening (2–3 Hours) – Practical Power Skills

Search & filters: grep, find, locate.

Archiving & compression: tar, gzip, zip.

Networking basics: ping, ip a, ss, curl, scp.

Shell scripting basics: loops, variables, conditionals.


✅ By the end of the day, you’ll:

Feel comfortable navigating Linux.

Manage files, processes, and users.

Run basic scripts and networking commands.


💡 Pro Tip: Practice in a virtual machine or WSL (Windows Subsystem for Linux) for hands-on experience.
